# Build context filter, consulted by BOTH worker/Dockerfile AND the main
# API's own Railway/Nixpacks build.
#
# CORRECTION (confirmed by an actual production build failure -- this is
# not theoretical): an earlier version of this file's own comment here
# claimed "the main API is Nixpacks-built ... this file has zero effect
# on the main API's build" -- that claim was WRONG. `builder = "NIXPACKS"`
# in railway.toml does not mean Docker/.dockerignore are bypassed:
# Nixpacks generates its OWN internal Dockerfile-equivalent build plan
# and still builds it through a standard Docker build context rooted at
# this repo -- and that build context is still filtered by THIS file.
# Railway build logs from the failed deploy confirmed this directly:
# Nixpacks' generated build plan does `COPY .nixpacks/nixpkgs-<hash>.nix
# .nixpacks/nixpkgs-<hash>.nix` (a file Nixpacks itself generates fresh
# into the build context at build time) -- and because an earlier version
# of the allow-list below did not include `.nixpacks/`, that COPY failed
# with "not found", and the entire production deploy failed. `.nixpacks/`
# is now explicitly allow-listed below specifically because of this.
#
# CAVEAT, still true and still worth restating: .dockerignore is scoped
# to the BUILD CONTEXT (this repo root), not to one Dockerfile -- if a
# root-level Dockerfile is ever added for the main API itself (building
# from this same repo-root context) INSTEAD of Nixpacks, this same
# allow-list would need api/, scraper/, intelligence/, etc. added too, or
# it would need re-scoping (e.g. Docker's per-Dockerfile
# `<name>.dockerignore` convention). The Nixpacks incident above is proof
# this file's effective scope is easy to underestimate -- verify empirically
# (as tests/unit/test_worker_deployment.py's dockerignore simulation test
# now does for the paths this file actually needs to affect), don't assume.
#
# This is an ALLOW-LIST, not a deny-list of currently-known clutter -- a
# deny-list only protects against files that exist today (this repo
# root's own real, present .env file; various *.csv/*.json data dumps;
# docs/; and many scratch/tooling directories like .agents/, .codex/,
# .cursor/, .ocr/, .schemathesis/, .serena/, .tkg/, .windsurf/, plus
# loose debug scripts). An allow-list protects against anything that
# shows up later too -- structurally, not by remembering to update this
# file every time something new lands at the repo root.
#
# worker/app.py's own import graph was traced by hand before writing this
# file (worker/ -> pipeline.company_enrichment.website_contact_provider
# -> db.models / pipeline.company_matching / pipeline.registry_verification
# -> db.registry_constants, db.market_registry_constants,
# db.company_canonical_constants, pipeline.company_resolution,
# pipeline.registry_verification.providers.* -> config.env, via
# db/__init__.py's own `from db.connection import ...`, which in turn
# imports `from config.env import get_env, load_app_env`). Four
# directories cover that whole graph: worker/, pipeline/, db/, config/.
#
# `.nixpacks/` is a fifth, different kind of entry: not a source
# directory worker/app.py imports, but Nixpacks' OWN generated build
# artifact (a `.nix` file it writes into the build context at build
# time, then immediately COPYs as part of its generated build plan --
# see the correction above). It must be allow-listed for the MAIN API's
# Nixpacks build to succeed at all, regardless of anything worker-related.
*

!worker/
!worker/**
!pipeline/
!pipeline/**
!db/
!db/**
!config/
!config/**
!.nixpacks/
!.nixpacks/**

# Re-deny bytecode/cache noise, and any stray env/secret file, inside the
# four allowed trees -- defense-in-depth. None of the four directories
# above contains any of these today (verified with `find worker pipeline
# db config -iname '*secret*' -o -iname '*credential*' -o -iname '*.pem'
# -o -iname '*.key'` before adding these lines -- zero matches), but the
# allow-list above only re-includes worker/, pipeline/, db/, config/ as a
# WHOLE -- it does nothing to stop a secret-shaped file dropped INSIDE
# one of those trees later from reaching the image, since it isn't
# outside the allow-list. These lines close that gap.
#
# .env in particular: config/env.py calls load_app_env() AT ITS OWN
# MODULE IMPORT TIME (config/env.py, top-level `load_app_env()` call
# outside any function), which silently loads whatever .env file it
# finds on disk (via python-dotenv's load_dotenv(), override=False) into
# the running process's environment -- merely importing db.models (which
# worker/app.py does transitively) is enough to trigger this. A .env file
# baked into this image, however it got there, would be loaded
# automatically at worker startup with zero further code involved. This
# repo's root .env (real, present today) must never reach the build
# context -- the allow-list above already keeps it out (it lives outside
# all four allowed trees), and these lines guard the hypothetical case of
# one appearing inside worker/, pipeline/, db/, or config/ later too.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/secrets/
**/*secret*
**/*credential*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
